concept imports {
  name = "Imports & Modules"
  summary = "Pull another file's declarations into the document — disk and system forms."
  related = [namespaces]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<DOC
      An `import` pulls another file's declarations into the current document. There are two forms,
      distinguished by how the path is written.
    DOC
    h2 "Disk imports"
    p "A quoted path imports a file from disk, resolved relative to the importing file."
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        import "./pages/values.wcl"
        import "../shared/types.wcl"
      DOC
    }
    h2 "System imports"
    p <<DOC
      An angle-bracket path imports a **system** module — a file provided by the host program
      through a registry, not the filesystem. wdoc's standard library is served this way: a single
      `import <wdoc.wcl>` pulls in the whole stdlib.
    DOC
    code "wcl" {
      source = "import <wdoc.wcl>\n"
    }
    callout "Import the wdoc stdlib once, at the root" {
      class = ["info"]
      body = "Start the document you pass to wcl wdoc build with import <wdoc.wcl>, and the stdlib is in scope there and in every page file it imports. Imported page files do not repeat the line."
    }
    h2 "How imports compose"
    p <<DOC
      Top-level imports are eager; an `import` inside a block is lazy. Imported declarations
      participate fully in the importer — in structural validation and name resolution — so a type
      or `let` declared in an imported file is usable as if local. An imported file keeps its own
      declaring `namespace`. See [Namespaces](concept_namespaces).
    DOC
    p <<DOC
      An `import` inside a block also splices the imported file's top-level block instances into the
      enclosing block as children — exactly as if written inline — so you can factor a nested
      subtree into its own file. The spliced instances are validated against the parent's `@child` /
      `@children` slots like any literal child.
    DOC
  }
}
